            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "brie9999"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Tasted just like my local Vietnamese restaurants. I did just use 1/2 cup water and 1/2 clove of garlic. I also added a bit of shredded carrot. I added the water hot.",
            "datePublished": "2010-02-03T16:57:08.233Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Grace"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Good recipe. So simple. Per other reviewers, I reduced the garlic to 1/2 a clove and reduced water to 1/2 a cup. Served with Vietnamese pork chops, rice, and fresh cucumber slices. Tasted very much like the restaurants. Now I can satisfy cravings at home!",
            "datePublished": "2007-01-18T04:54:07.040Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "JOSIE"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I am by no means a fish sauce person, but I can tolerate it when made like this. We served this with fried gourd and certainly a little goes a long way. I would suggest adding a little pickled ginger (chop up fresh ginger into matchstrips and stuff into a jar...add enough regular vinegar to cover and let sit at least 30 minutes) to give it an extra layer of flavor...but it's good as is. Thanks Maryellen.",
            "datePublished": "2004-02-12T09:33:02.437Z"
          },
          {
